## Two records: the tracker and the knowledge base

`docs/agile/` is **what we are doing**. `docs/knowledge/` is **what we know**. A
ticket is closed and forgotten; a note outlives it. Both are plain Markdown
committed here, both have a generated `INDEX.md` you must never edit, and both
have a validator that exits 2 when they drift.

`forge.json` is the third record: **what this project is shaped like** — its
repositories, scopes, owning agents and runnable checks. Read it rather than
assuming:

```bash
python3 .claude/scripts/forge.py scopes
python3 .claude/scripts/forge.py checks <scope>
python3 .claude/scripts/forge.py doctor
```

## Work goes through the tracker

Read [`docs/agile/README.md`](docs/agile/README.md) before starting anything;
[`docs/agile/SCHEMA.md`](docs/agile/SCHEMA.md) is the specification.

```
/agile:board            what is going on
/agile:groom <request>  turn a request into tickets
/agile:next             what to work on
/agile:work <ID>        QA writes failing tests → implement → QA reviews, stops at verify
/agile:close <ID>       merge, record the sha, close
/agile:bug <symptom>    file and triage a defect
```

## Knowledge goes through the knowledge base

```
docs/knowledge/<scope>/<type>/{<type>} <description> - <yyyy-mm-dd>.md
```

`<type>` is one of `business-rule`, `troubleshooting`, `overview`,
`integration`, `api-contract`, `decision`, `runbook`, `data-model`. **The braces
around the type are literal**, the description is lowercase words separated by
spaces, and the created date follows a ` - ` separator. Paths therefore need
quoting — let `kb.py` print them rather than retyping. The specification is
[`docs/knowledge/README.md`](docs/knowledge/README.md); the procedure the agents
follow is `.claude/skills/project-knowledge/SKILL.md`.

```
/kb:consult <subject>  what do we already know?
/kb:new <finding>      file it, correctly typed and cross-linked
/kb:audit              gaps, misfilings, staleness, broken links
/kb:lint               validate the knowledge base and the tracker's links into it

python3 .claude/scripts/kb.py find <keyword> [...]     # search, exit 2 = a gap
python3 .claude/scripts/kb.py for-ticket <TICKET-ID>   # the ticket's reading list
python3 .claude/scripts/kb.py new <type> <scope> "<description>" --ticket <ID>
python3 .claude/scripts/kb.py related "<basename>"     # one hop along the graph
```

**Consulting the knowledge base is a step of the cycle, not optional reading.**
Before changing business logic, an integration, a domain workflow or the
architecture, search it. After the work reveals something durable, file it — one
finding per note, under the right type, cross-linked in `## Related` in both
directions. Where a note and the code disagree, **the code wins**: say so, and
fix the note.
